module

public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.ClosedImmersionFinitePresentation.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Flatness.SubschemeFiltered

/-!
# A closed subscheme as the limit of its finite-presentation enlargements

Fix a scheme `Y` and a quasi-coherent ideal sheaf `K` on it. The ideal sheaves `J ≤ K` whose
closed subscheme is of finite presentation over `Y` form a directed set, indexing an inverse
system of closed subschemes of `Y` all containing the one cut out by `K`. The closed subscheme
cut out by `K` receives a canonical cone on that system, and this file shows that cone is a
limit **as soon as `K` is the supremum of those `J`**, which is the only remaining input.

That supremum condition is Properties, Tag 01PG read at a sheaf of ideals: every quasi-coherent
sheaf of ideals on a quasi-compact and quasi-separated scheme is the directed union of its
sheaves of ideals of finite type. It is named `IsSupOfFinitePresentation` here and proved only
for an affine scheme, where the lattice of ideal sheaves is the lattice of ideals of the global
sections and every ideal is the union of the ideals generated by its finite subsets.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.IsSupOfFinitePresentation`: the condition that `K`
  is the supremum of the ideal sheaves below it cutting out finite-presentation closed
  subschemes.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.toFinitePresentationStage`: the map from a closed subscheme to each of its
  finite-presentation enlargements.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.finitePresentationCone`: the cone, with vertex the given closed immersion,
  on the inverse system of its finite-presentation enlargements.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.isLimit_finitePresentationCone`: the cone is a limit in `Over Y`. With the
  three facts beside it — the index is cofiltered
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.isCofiltered_finitePresentationSubscheme_op`), each stage is a closed
  immersion of finite presentation
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.finitePresentation_subschemeDiagram_obj`), and the transition morphisms
  are closed immersions (`AlgebraicGeometry.isClosedImmersion_subschemeDiagram_map`) — this is
  Limits, Tag 09ZP under the hypothesis `IsSupOfFinitePresentation`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.isSupOfFinitePresentation_of_isAffine`: the hypothesis holds on an affine
  scheme.

## Implementation notes

No smallness or finiteness hypothesis enters the limit statement. The reason is the asymmetry
recorded elsewhere in this tree: an *eventual* statement about a limit needs a cofinality
bound, while *exhibiting a cone as limiting* does not. Here both the universal property and
its uniqueness clause are free, because `K.subschemeι` is a monomorphism: any two morphisms
into an object of `Over Y` whose structure morphism is a monomorphism are equal
(`AlgebraicGeometry.subsingleton_over_hom_of_mono`), so the `fac` and `uniq` fields of the
limit are discharged by `Subsingleton.elim` and the whole content is the existence of the lift.
That lift is Mathlib's universal property of closed immersions,
`AlgebraicGeometry.IsClosedImmersion.lift`, whose hypothesis `K ≤ (cone vertex).hom.ker` is
exactly where the supremum condition is consumed.

`@[stacks 09ZP]` is **withheld here**, every result in this file carrying either
`IsSupOfFinitePresentation` or `IsAffine` as a hypothesis, which the tag does not. It sits on
`AlgebraicGeometry.isLimit_finitePresentationCone_of_qcqs`, in
`StacksDraft.AlgebraicGeometry.F3b.IdealSheafFiniteType.Basic`, where the hypothesis is proved.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09ZP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09ZP)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01PG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PG)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0EV9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EV9)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits AlgebraicGeometry Stacks.Flatness

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Two morphisms of `Over Y` with the same source and with target an object whose structure
morphism is a monomorphism are equal. -/
theorem subsingleton_over_hom_of_mono {Y Z : Scheme.{u}} (g : Z ⟶ Y) [Mono g] (A : Over Y) :
    Subsingleton (A ⟶ Over.mk g) :=
  ⟨fun p q ↦ Over.OverMorphism.ext (by
    rw [← cancel_mono g]; exact (Over.w p).trans (Over.w q).symm)⟩

/-- A quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals is the supremum of the sheaves of ideals below it which
cut out closed subschemes of finite presentation. This is Properties, Tag 01PG read at a sheaf
of ideals; the reverse inequality `iSup_le_isSupOfFinitePresentation` is unconditional, so the
condition is the inequality `K ≤ ⨆ J`. -/
abbrev Scheme.IdealSheafData.IsSupOfFinitePresentation {Y : Scheme.{u}}
    (K : Y.IdealSheafData) : Prop :=
  (⨆ J : FinitePresentationSubscheme K, J.1) = K

variable {Y Z : Scheme.{u}} {K : Y.IdealSheafData}

/-- The supremum of the finite-presentation enlargements of a closed subscheme is always below
its ideal sheaf; the content of `IsSupOfFinitePresentation` is the other inequality. -/
theorem iSup_le_of_finitePresentationSubscheme (K : Y.IdealSheafData) :
    (⨆ J : FinitePresentationSubscheme K, J.1) ≤ K :=
  iSup_le fun J ↦ J.2.2

/-- `IsSupOfFinitePresentation` reduces to an assertion about single sections: it is enough
that every section of `K` over an affine open already lies in some finitely generated ideal
sheaf below `K`. This is the elementwise form of Properties, Tag 01PG at a sheaf of ideals, and
it is the shape in which Tag 01PF would be applied. -/
theorem isSupOfFinitePresentation_of_forall_mem (K : Y.IdealSheafData)
    (h : ∀ (U : Y.affineOpens) (x : Γ(Y, U.1)), x ∈ K.ideal U →
      ∃ J : Y.IdealSheafData, FinitePresentation J.subschemeι ∧ J ≤ K ∧ x ∈ J.ideal U) :
    K.IsSupOfFinitePresentation := by
  refine le_antisymm (iSup_le_of_finitePresentationSubscheme K)
    (Scheme.IdealSheafData.le_of_iSup_eq_top (fun U : Y.affineOpens ↦ U)
      (iSup_affineOpens_eq_top Y) fun U x hx ↦ ?_)
  obtain ⟨J, hJfp, hJle, hJmem⟩ := h U x hx
  rw [Scheme.IdealSheafData.ideal_iSup, iSup_apply]
  exact Submodule.mem_iSup_of_mem ⟨J, hJfp, hJle⟩ hJmem

/-- The index category of the inverse system of finite-presentation enlargements is
cofiltered. -/
theorem isCofiltered_finitePresentationSubscheme_op (K : Y.IdealSheafData) :
    IsCofiltered (FinitePresentationSubscheme K)ᵒᵖ :=
  have := finitePresentationSubscheme_isFiltered K
  inferInstance

/-- Each stage of the inverse system is a closed immersion into `Y` of finite presentation. -/
theorem finitePresentation_subschemeDiagram_obj (K : Y.IdealSheafData)
    (J : (FinitePresentationSubscheme K)ᵒᵖ) :
    FinitePresentation ((subschemeDiagram K).obj J).hom :=
  J.unop.2.1

/-- Each stage of the inverse system is a closed immersion into `Y`. -/
instance isClosedImmersion_subschemeDiagram_obj (K : Y.IdealSheafData)
    (J : (FinitePresentationSubscheme K)ᵒᵖ) :
    IsClosedImmersion ((subschemeDiagram K).obj J).hom :=
  inferInstanceAs (IsClosedImmersion (J.unop.1).subschemeι)

/-- The transition morphisms of the inverse system are closed immersions. -/
theorem isClosedImmersion_subschemeDiagram_map (K : Y.IdealSheafData)
    {J H : (FinitePresentationSubscheme K)ᵒᵖ} (p : J ⟶ H) :
    IsClosedImmersion ((subschemeDiagram K).map p).left := by
  have hcomp : ((subschemeDiagram K).map p).left ≫ ((subschemeDiagram K).obj H).hom =
      ((subschemeDiagram K).obj J).hom := Over.w ((subschemeDiagram K).map p)
  have : IsClosedImmersion (((subschemeDiagram K).map p).left ≫
      ((subschemeDiagram K).obj H).hom) := hcomp ▸ inferInstance
  exact IsClosedImmersion.of_comp_isClosedImmersion _ ((subschemeDiagram K).obj H).hom

/-- The canonical map from a closed subscheme to one of its finite-presentation
enlargements. -/
def toFinitePresentationStage (g : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion g] (hg : K ≤ g.ker)
    (J : FinitePresentationSubscheme K) : Z ⟶ J.1.subscheme :=
  IsClosedImmersion.lift J.1.subschemeι g
    ((Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_subschemeι J.1).le.trans (J.2.2.trans hg))

/-- The map `toFinitePresentationStage g hg J` composed with the closed immersion of the stage
`J` into `Y` is `g`. -/
@[simp]
theorem toFinitePresentationStage_fac (g : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion g] (hg : K ≤ g.ker)
    (J : FinitePresentationSubscheme K) :
    toFinitePresentationStage g hg J ≫ J.1.subschemeι = g :=
  IsClosedImmersion.lift_fac _ _ _

/-- The leg of `AlgebraicGeometry.finitePresentationCone` at one stage. -/
def finitePresentationConeLeg (g : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion g] (hg : K ≤ g.ker)
    (J : (FinitePresentationSubscheme K)ᵒᵖ) :
    (Over.mk g : Over Y) ⟶ (subschemeDiagram K).obj J :=
  Over.homMk (toFinitePresentationStage g hg J.unop)
    (by simp [subschemeDiagram])

/-- The cone, with vertex a closed immersion whose ideal sheaf dominates `K`, on the inverse
system of finite-presentation closed subschemes above `K`. -/
def finitePresentationCone (g : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion g] (hg : K ≤ g.ker) :
    Cone (subschemeDiagram K) where
  pt := Over.mk g
  π :=
    { app := finitePresentationConeLeg g hg
      naturality := fun _ _ _ ↦ (subsingleton_over_hom_of_mono _ _).elim _ _ }

/-- Every stage of the inverse system dominates the ideal sheaf of the vertex of any cone. -/
theorem le_ker_cone_pt (c : Cone (subschemeDiagram K)) (J : FinitePresentationSubscheme K) :
    J.1 ≤ c.pt.hom.ker := by
  have hw : (c.π.app (Opposite.op J)).left ≫ J.1.subschemeι = c.pt.hom :=
    Over.w (c.π.app (Opposite.op J))
  have hker : J.1.subschemeι.ker = J.1 := Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_subschemeι J.1
  have h : J.1.subschemeι.ker ≤ ((c.π.app (Opposite.op J)).left ≫ J.1.subschemeι).ker :=
    Scheme.Hom.le_ker_comp _ _
  rw [hker, hw] at h
  exact h

/-- The lift of an arbitrary cone to the vertex of `finitePresentationCone`. -/
def finitePresentationConeLift (g : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion g]
    (hK : K.IsSupOfFinitePresentation) (hgK : g.ker ≤ K)
    (c : Cone (subschemeDiagram K)) : c.pt.left ⟶ Z :=
  IsClosedImmersion.lift g c.pt.hom
    (hgK.trans (hK.ge.trans (iSup_le fun J ↦ le_ker_cone_pt c J)))

/-- The map `finitePresentationConeLift g hK hgK c` composed with `g` is the structure morphism
of the vertex of `c`. -/
@[simp]
theorem finitePresentationConeLift_fac (g : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion g]
    (hK : K.IsSupOfFinitePresentation) (hgK : g.ker ≤ K)
    (c : Cone (subschemeDiagram K)) :
    finitePresentationConeLift g hK hgK c ≫ g = c.pt.hom :=
  IsClosedImmersion.lift_fac _ _ _

/-- **Limits, Tag 09ZP, under the hypothesis `IsSupOfFinitePresentation`.** A closed subscheme
of `Y` whose ideal sheaf is `K` is the limit, in `Over Y`, of the inverse system of
finite-presentation closed subschemes of `Y` above it. -/
def isLimit_finitePresentationCone (g : Z ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion g] (hg : K ≤ g.ker)
    (hgK : g.ker ≤ K) (hK : K.IsSupOfFinitePresentation) :
    IsLimit (finitePresentationCone g hg) where
  lift c := Over.homMk (finitePresentationConeLift g hK hgK c)
    (by simp [finitePresentationCone])
  fac _ _ := (subsingleton_over_hom_of_mono _ _).elim _ _
  uniq _ _ _ := (subsingleton_over_hom_of_mono _ _).elim _ _

/-- The form of `isLimit_finitePresentationCone` taken at the kernel of the closed immersion
itself, which is how Tag 09ZP is stated: the source of a closed immersion `f` is the limit of
the finite-presentation closed subschemes of the target containing it. -/
def isLimit_finitePresentationCone_ker {X : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion f]
    (hf : f.ker.IsSupOfFinitePresentation) :
    IsLimit (finitePresentationCone f (le_refl f.ker)) :=
  isLimit_finitePresentationCone f (le_refl f.ker) (le_refl f.ker) hf

/-- The closed subscheme cut out by a finitely generated ideal of the global sections is of
finite presentation over the scheme. -/
theorem finitePresentation_subschemeι_ofIdealTop {a : Ideal Γ(Y, ⊤)} (ha : a.FG) :
    FinitePresentation (Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop a).subschemeι :=
  (finitePresentation_subschemeι_iff _).mpr fun U ↦ by
    rw [Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop_ideal]
    exact ha.map _

/-- On an affine scheme a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals is the supremum of the sheaves of
ideals below it which are of finite type: ideal sheaves are ideals of the global sections, and
every ideal is the supremum of the ideals generated by one element. -/
theorem isSupOfFinitePresentation_of_isAffine [IsAffine Y] (K : Y.IdealSheafData) :
    K.IsSupOfFinitePresentation := by
  refine le_antisymm (iSup_le_of_finitePresentationSubscheme K)
    (Scheme.IdealSheafData.le_of_isAffine ?_)
  rw [Scheme.IdealSheafData.ideal_iSup, iSup_apply]
  intro x hx
  have hax : Ideal.span {x} ≤ K.ideal ⟨⊤, isAffineOpen_top Y⟩ := by
    rw [Ideal.span_le]; simpa using hx
  have hfg : (Ideal.span {x} : Ideal Γ(Y, ⊤)).FG := Submodule.fg_span_singleton x
  have htop : (Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop (Ideal.span {x})).ideal
      ⟨⊤, isAffineOpen_top Y⟩ = Ideal.span {x} := by
    rw [Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop_ideal]
    simp
  refine Submodule.mem_iSup_of_mem
    ⟨Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop (Ideal.span {x}),
      finitePresentation_subschemeι_ofIdealTop hfg,
      Scheme.IdealSheafData.le_of_isAffine (htop.trans_le hax)⟩ ?_
  rw [htop]
  exact Ideal.subset_span rfl

/-- Tag 09ZP for an affine target: a closed subscheme of an affine scheme is the limit of the
finite-presentation closed subschemes containing it. -/
def isLimit_finitePresentationCone_of_isAffine {X : Scheme.{u}} [IsAffine Y] (f : X ⟶ Y)
    [IsClosedImmersion f] : IsLimit (finitePresentationCone f (le_refl f.ker)) :=
  isLimit_finitePresentationCone_ker f (isSupOfFinitePresentation_of_isAffine f.ker)

end AlgebraicGeometry

end
